  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Not as cold tonight with clear skies and light\nto calm winds. Dry for most through Monday Night.\n\nCenter of a high pressure reaches the Maritimes this evening,\nbut is still strong enough to dominate our weather through\nMonday. Then southern new England is stuck between a high\npressure over the North Atlantic and another over Ohio. This\nslight weakness in the pressure pattern opens the door for a low\npressure off the Mid Atlantic coast to nudge slightly farther \nnorth late Monday into Monday night.\n\nFor most of our region, dry weather will continue through Monday \nnight with a slight warming trends. The exception will be \ntowards the outer Cape and Nantucket, where some showers are\npossible Monday evening and night. Still, there will be plenty\nof dry hours there as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing chances for rainfall across coastal\nareas for this upcoming work week as a offshore Storm gradually \napproaches from the south. The system likely brings significant \nmarine impacts in the form of strong winds, rough seas, and\nbeach erosion through much of the upcoming week.\n\nConfidence is somewhat lower than normal for the eventual track\nand intensity of this week's nor'easter. While the majority of\nguidance tracks the low center well to the S of the 40/70\nbenchmark, there has been a trend NW amongst AI, high res, and\nan increasing number of global guidance. This subtle NW jog is\nlikely driven by the interaction between the cutoff low driving \nthe nor'easter and an additional shortwave trough moving S from \nCanada by later Wednesday. The main change has been to amplify\nthe shortwave from a flat/positive tilt to more of a neutral\ntilt. This positioning allows for a stronger more amplified\nsystem and thus a track further N. In terms of QPF, the\nnorthward trend has brought a notable uptick in the probability \nof >0.5\" of rainfall along and S of the I-95 corridor. As of the \n12z suite, most models show a 40-60% chance of rain totals in\nthis range. There are still some models, namely the NAM and AI\nguidance that show a track even further north and the chance for \na soaking rain event for much of the region. This is certainly a\nlow probability event but nonetheless a possibility at this \ntime.\n\nThe higher confidence aspect of the forecast continues to be\nsignificant marine impacts driven by increasing NE winds. Low\npressure to the S will move against high pressure over southern \nQuebec later Monday. The resulting pressure gradient will bring \nan increasing likelihood of Gale force winds well to the north \nof the actual surface low. Winds build up towards Monday night \nas the low pressure deepens and drifts to the north. Ensembles \nhave shown near 80-90 percent probabilities of sustained winds \nreaching gale-force over the southern waters despite a low track\nto the S of the benchmark. Thus, we opted to upgrade Gale\nWatches to Gale Warnings for the outer waters and have\nintroduced Gale Watches for the near coastal Waters. Finally, we \nwill have to monitor the chance for significant beach erosion as\nwell with a prolonged easterly fetch of winds and waves through\nthe week.\n\nLater this week, the aforementioned trough likely pushes the\ncoastal low farther offshore putting an end to any lingering\nimpacts. Significant ridging aloft over much of the CONUS looks \nto persist and even intensify, then becomes more tilted towards \nthe Great Lakes region towards the weekend. This would be\nassociated with a strong Canadian high pressure that looks to\nstrengthen over the northeast after originating from the\nCanadian Prairies, which would lean towards drier conditions for \nthe whole region once again.",
